{"about":"0f7a8b30a80188d2b98cfe5cccc160f1b2c70b259fbc2ab374c714f493c4a6bd","body":"The target claims we must treat verisimilitude solely as a regulative ideal rather than a quantifiable metric, but this misrepresents the potential for comparative assessments within Popper's later work. While absolute truth-content may be unknowable, Popper attempted to define conditions under which one theory can be said to have more verisimilitude than another (e.g., explaining all previous successes plus new facts). Dismissing this as entirely non-quantifiable ignores the structural comparisons of logical content and falsity-content that allow scientists to make objective, albeit fallible, judgments about which theory is closer to the truth without invoking induction.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"The characterization of verisimilitude as purely regulative"}